! forms-rule-result

`[function[forms-rule-result],<rules>,<values>]` — the result of the //first// rule in a table that a set of values satisfies, or nothing when none does.

* `rules` — title of a JSON data tiddler holding the table (below).
* `values` — a title list: the values to test, typically the tags of some tiddler.

```
{"rules": [
  {"all": ["round", "red"], "result": "tomato"},
  {"all": ["round"], "none": ["red", "orange"], "result": "ball"},
  {"result": "something else"}
]}
```

A rule applies when every value in its `all` is present and no value in its `none` is. Either may be left out, so a rule with neither always applies and makes a default when it comes last. Rules are tried in order and the first that applies wins, which is what lets a specific rule sit above the general one it refines. Comparison ignores case, so a table need not list every spelling of a value.

The point of a table is that the mapping is data: adding a case, or reordering two, is an edit to a tiddler that a configuration layer ships and a user may override, not a change to a filter.

When nothing applies the result is empty. A caller that needs a value should say so — a form does it by marking a derived value `required` (see [[$:/plugins/tiddlywiki/forms/form]]) — rather than assume one.

The helper functions read the outer call's `rules` and `values` parameters, so they are not meant to be called on their own.
